{%- comment -%}
  Arabic typography.

  The theme let the merchant pick a Latin font from Shopify's library and then
  fell back to whatever Arabic face the phone happened to have — which on most
  Android devices is a default Naskh that was never drawn for a dense commerce
  UI. Arabic was the one language this theme is sold into that had no typeface
  of its own.

  Two things make this cheap enough to ship by default:

  The faces are declared with unicode-range covering Arabic only, so a shop
  running in French never downloads a single byte of them — the browser skips
  a face whose range no glyph on the page falls into. And Cairo is a variable
  font: one 30KB file carries every weight from 200 to 1000, so the theme is
  not shipping four static files to get regular, semibold and extrabold.

  The Latin stack is untouched: the merchant's Shopify font still sets French
  and every numeral. Only Arabic letters change face, which is what keeps a
  bilingual page — an Arabic label beside a Latin price — looking deliberate
  rather than like two websites.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign ar_font = settings.arabic_font | default: 'cairo'
-%}

{% style %}
  {%- comment -%}
    The range is the Arabic block plus its supplements, presentation forms,
    and the zero-width joiners the script needs to connect letters. Anything
    outside it — Latin, digits, punctuation — is left to the Latin stack.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ar_range = 'U+0600-06FF, U+0750-077F, U+0870-088E, U+0890-0891, U+08A0-08FF, U+200C-200E, U+2010-2011, U+204F, U+2E41, U+FB50-FDFF, U+FE70-FE74, U+FE76-FEFC' -%}

  {%- if ar_font == 'cairo' -%}
    @font-face {
      font-family: "Souq Arabic";
      src: url({{ 'cairo-arabic.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
      font-weight: 200 1000;
      font-style: normal;
      font-display: swap;
      unicode-range: {{ ar_range }};
    }
  {%- elsif ar_font == 'tajawal' -%}
    @font-face {
      font-family: "Souq Arabic";
      src: url({{ 'tajawal-arabic-400.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
      font-weight: 400;
      font-style: normal;
      font-display: swap;
      unicode-range: {{ ar_range }};
    }
    @font-face {
      font-family: "Souq Arabic";
      src: url({{ 'tajawal-arabic-700.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
      font-weight: 700 900;
      font-style: normal;
      font-display: swap;
      unicode-range: {{ ar_range }};
    }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if ar_font != 'system' -%}
    :root {
      /* First in the stack, but it only claims the Arabic range, so Latin
         falls straight through to the merchant's own font. */
      --f-body: "Souq Arabic", {{ settings.type_body.family | default: 'Inter' }},
        {{ settings.type_body.fallback_families | default: 'sans-serif' }};
      --f-heading: "Souq Arabic", {{ settings.type_heading.family | default: 'Inter' }},
        {{ settings.type_heading.fallback_families | default: 'sans-serif' }};
    }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Corrections that matter more than the face itself.

    Negative tracking is the default in this theme's headings, and on Arabic
    it is not a style choice — it pulls joined letters into each other and
    damages the word. Arabic letterforms also sit on a deeper baseline with
    ascenders and descenders that collide at Latin line-heights.

    These are scoped to the RTL storefront so a French shop keeps its tighter
    Latin setting untouched.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  [dir="rtl"] body,
  [dir="rtl"] h1, [dir="rtl"] h2, [dir="rtl"] h3,
  [dir="rtl"] h4, [dir="rtl"] h5, [dir="rtl"] h6,
  [dir="rtl"] .bb__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .hero-w__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .ty__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .bb__price-now,
  [dir="rtl"] .pdetails__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .drawer__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .cod__title {
    letter-spacing: normal;
  }

  [dir="rtl"] body { line-height: 1.75; }
  [dir="rtl"] h1, [dir="rtl"] h2, [dir="rtl"] h3 { line-height: 1.4; }
  [dir="rtl"] .trow__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .pgc__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .cpill { line-height: 1.55; }

  {%- comment -%}
    Arabic has no capitals, so uppercasing does nothing to Arabic text — but
    it still applies to any Latin mixed into the same label, which makes a
    bilingual eyebrow read as two shouting registers.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  [dir="rtl"] .hero-w__eyebrow { text-transform: none; letter-spacing: normal; }

  {%- comment -%}
    An Arabic word cannot be hyphenated and its glyphs are wider at the same
    point size, so the small dense labels need a touch more room before they
    truncate. Kashida justification is deliberately left off: browsers
    implement it inconsistently and it stretches words unevenly at these sizes.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  [dir="rtl"] .pgc__title,
  [dir="rtl"] .bb__title { word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
{% endstyle %}
